tunity to go and work with somebody  in Hawaii and we moved our family and  lived there on Kauai for three months on  an organic farm and this guy paid us to  do a number of things and it was like  thank God you know and that's where I  started the podcast and I had no idea  that the podcast would become what it  has become but it was sort of just a  tuning your attention to the universe  trying to pay attention to what is  showing up and being grateful for that  walking forward with gratitude and  ultimately the universe did conspire to  support us it didn't happen on the time  frame that I would have liked and it  didn't show up in the manner that I  imagined but that edict has proven true  again in my life yeah so would you say  that doubt was the ultimate test of  faith during that time like did you face  a lot of I faced a lot of doubt you know  doubt was doubt doubt was a recurring  theme and in in my journey and I think  that that's okay to doubt is to be human  if you're not doubting yourself along  the way perhaps you're not extending  yourself far enough perhaps you're not  pushing yourself far enough because when  you extend yourself and you're out of  your comfort zone and the path forward  is unclear and the terrain is is is not  well-worn  it can be confusing and destabilizing  and that's what creates doubt you know  when I look back on my life it all looks  like it lined up perfectly like of  course I'm here because I did all these  things and it all makes sense but when  you're in the midst of it you don't it I  just I just did a podcast with the free  solo climber Alex Honnold who's the guy  who scaled El Capitan without any ropes  like he's unbelievable and he said  something like I'm probably gonna  butcher the quote but he said something  like when you're in the middle of it  when you're in the middle of like trying  to find  path you don't always know that you're  on a path right you don't you know if  you don't know what the next step is  because there's no example and you're  trying to figure it out on your own  then of course you're gonna have moments  of doubt you're gonna be tested right  but I think if you're not being tested  like I said you're not you're not  pushing yourself hard enough to be  tested means that doubt will be provoked  and it's it's not it's not about  avoiding doubt or avoiding fear it's how  you respond to those things  when those things crop up do you move  forward with conviction and purpose or  do you cower in fear and retreat mmm  right and there were many moments where  I thought it's time to retreat I can't  go any further I can't do this anymore  maybe if I was by myself living in a hut  I could do it but there's other people  in my life I have four kids I have you  know responsibilities and as a head of  household  it can be very emasculating to have this  vision for your life this sense of  purpose and when it's not coming  together and it's not working out and  other people are being you know are part  of this grand play and you feel like  you're not doing your job as a husband  and as a father it's it's a it's a  terrifying thing you know and I've  definitely endured that and I think  without my wife who who supported me the  entire time and kept telling me you know  keep moving forward I probably would  have retreated because I think most  partners would say enough of this  nonsense like you need to go make some  cash you know go get a job what are you  doing you're acting like a crazy person  and I could tell you there were a lot of  naysayers a lot of people thought Julie  and I were crazy a lot of people said to  either to our faces or behind our backs  like what are they doing like what I  don't understand he's so talented he's  so smart he has all these skills why is  he wasting it doing this kind of thing  and that plays on your psyche you know  if you allow it to so developing the the  awareness and the conviction and the  faith was really the work  and so for me it's an inside job  it's about your relationship with  yourself and how committed can you be to  this vision that you have for your life  let's talk about going into becoming  Ultraman and the Ironman challenge and  how he got into that was that a way for  you to cope with all this uncertainty or  was that a catalyst for you to become  who you are today  I think both you know when I first  decided I was gonna try to compete in  this Ultraman race and we should say for  you know people that are watching  Ultraman is a double Ironman distance  triathlon the world championship version  of which is held in Hawaii  every November it's it's a three-day  event in which you circumnavigate the  entire Big Island of Hawaii so the first  day is a 6.2 miles swim followed by a 90  mile bike the second day is 171 miles on  the bike and the third day is a 52 mile  run double marathon run it's absolutely  bananas insane it's only 35 competitors  they keep it very small there's no media  there's no press there's no prize money  there's no attention it's very  old-school and in many ways what  attracted me to it was that it was  almost like more than an athletic event  it was like this spiritual Odyssey this  this this adventure that will take you  on this journey at the end of which you  will be transformed in some way and  spiritually mentally emotionally and  physically and I was attracted to it  because I was because I was starting to  integrate into my body as a result of  this change to eating plant-based and I  was feeling really good and and I wanted  I wanted a physical challenge I that was  so much a part of my life growing up as  a kid was something I loved I mean I  then fell out of love with it and I  realized when I started to think like  what makes me happy what do I want to do  it was very simple things it was like I  love jumping in a swimming pool I love  the feeling of the Sun hitting my  shoulder you know  on a trail run at dawn like those are  the things that make me happy and I just  made this very conscious decision that I  was going to start doing things that  made me happy because my career wasn't  making me happy and I was so tired of  trying to jam this square peg into a  round hole that I kind of almost gave up  and said I'm just gonna be Who I am  and that doesn't mean that it's a career  path but I'm still gonna find a way to  bring expression to this into my life  and then one thing led to another and  then this is what I wanted to explore I  felt like this was a great template to  test the outer limits of my potential as  an athlete something that I had not  realized that the swimmer at Stanford  because of my alcoholism so there was a  bit of unfinished business but I also  saw it as a like a crucible for personal  growth  I had these questions about what I  wanted to do with myself Who am I what  is my purpose and there's something  about the ultra-endurance world when  you're doing these events you become so  depleted you are tested so  comprehensively that you're really  forced to confront yourself in a very  real way all the denial strips away and  you're faced with who you really are and  that was what I sought through that  experience and the experiences that  followed and that's what I got so I feel  like it gave me a North Star and an  anchor and a sense of direction now how  that translates into like the career  that I have today is very intangible and  it's a whole other conversation but  that's what lured me to it and  ultimately it delivered on its promise  yeah it was really an internal and  internal Drive I just I became acutely  aware of how unhappy I was and then I  had a choice and if I wanted to be happy  that that journey would have to begin  with me making some difficult decisions  about how I was spending my time and  energy and it always reminds me of this  quote by Henry David Thoreau he said the  mass of men lead lives of quiet  desperation and what is considered  resignation is confirmed desperation  now he said that in whenever it was 1880  or I I don't remember exactly when leads  to grass came out but I think it's very  apropos to what is happening right now I  think we're in a cultural crisis a  spiritual crisis where a lot of people  perhaps even the majority of people are  stuck in lives and careers that they  find unfulfilled  robotic aliy reacting to the world  around us we're responding to social and  cultural expectations and pressures and  we're not living our lives purposefully  or mindfully and there's so much energy  into maintaining that system that to  step outside of it and question it is to  become a pariah and also to be  potentially you know ostracized by  family and friends and it takes a lot of  courage you know and so out of  desperation and pain I was able to step  outside of that and pursue a new way and  my whole message now and kind of why I  do what I do is to help people  understand that they too have a choice  that that what I was able to do and  accomplish is my own unique personal  journey but it is it is an allegory for  I think the hero's journey that we all  have locked inside ourselves to live or  live our lives in a more mindful  purposeful way not everyone wants to be  an ultra endurance athlete or go and do  ultraman I get that that's my version of  this story but I think everybody has  some passion or some unique creative  expression inside of them that is  probably locked down deep perhaps even  all the way down into their unconscious  mind that if they could find a way to  give that birth it would gird their  lives with a little bit more meaning and  a little bit more direction and a little  bit  more happiness speaking of meaning what  is your definition of success I think  success is the freedom to have domain  over your time and your energy and your  resources and to make choices that are  in alignment with how you want to live  your life success isn't a financial  number I know we live in Los Angeles I  know a lot of rich people that are very  unhappy there are prisoners of their  careers and their their account balances  real success is the freedom to have  choice and dominion over how you spend  your time  so why veganism why plant-based well it  began really with just trying to find a  way to eat that agreed with me that  allowed me to feel good throughout the  day without all kinds of energy lulls  and without crazy food cravings and I  tried a bunch of diets and honestly I  didn't want to become plant-based it  seemed really extreme and like some kind  of crazy deprivation protocol but when I  finally had explored every other option  except for going plant-based I did it on  a last resort almost to prove that it  wouldn't work  and within seven to ten days of eating  nothing but plants nothing without a  mother nothing with a face just whole  plant-based foods close to their natural  state it had a huge impact on how I felt  it just really revitalized me in a very  rapid period of time so my initial  impetus and the reason that I got into  it was really just personal like I was  tired of being fat and tired and lazy I  didn't want to have a gut you know I  didn't I didn't want to look older than  I was at the time and now I've been  plant-based for over 11 years and my  relationship with food continues to  evolve but I've never been more  enthusiastic about being a vegan  plant-based individual and athlete  because I think that it is a lifestyle  that checks a couple very important  boxes for me  we talked about this sort of malaise of  just dissatisfaction that that is kind  of happening culturally right now while  simultaneously despite the fact that  we're an incredibly prosperous country  we've never been more sick as a society  right one out of every three people will  die of a heart attack  that's insane when you think about it by  2030 50 percent of Americans are gonna  be diabetic or pre-diabetic obesity  rates are through the roof I think 70  percent of people are overweight or  obese childhood obesity rates on the  rise so we have a gigantic healthcare  crisis on our hands and this epidemic of  chronic lifestyle illness is not only  affecting our the health of ourselves  we're also exporting it across the world  and it's costing us trillions of dollars  it's bankrupting our economy so what is  going on we need to reframe our  relationship with food and the truth of  the matter is when you go plant-based it  is the one dietary protocol that's shown  to not only prevent heart disease but  actually reverse people that have heart  disease it is the antidote to type 2  diabetes people who have type 2 diabetes  they go plant-based they reverse their  conditions it's a way to maintain your  optimal body weight and then even  perhaps the larger issue at hand is the  environmental implications of the food  choices that we make animal agriculture  is devastating our environment at an  almost unfathomable rate every second  one to two football-field-sized parcels  of land are cleared in the Amazonian  rainforest to raise crops to feed cattle  every second every second it's insane  and I think the stat is something like  forty five percent of all arable land  right now is used for either cattle  grazing or to raise crops to feed these  cattle it's an unbelievably inefficient  system that is creating waste so profuse  it's creating algal blooms and are in  our oceans it's polluting our water  table it's creating the biggest mass  species extinction event  you know basically the history of  mankind in terms of it being man-made  and created the the carbon emissions  from animal agriculture are are more  profound than all the transportation  sector combined the amount of land and  water and resources that go into raising  these animals for food it doesn't make  sense  it's insane and when you go plant-based  you opt out of that system altogether  not only are you doing that you're also  sparing all these animals lives these  animals that are treated horribly that  are slaughtered that are tortured and  all the like so for me it is it's a it's  a beautiful ethical way to live it's not  it's not like a zero impact lifestyle  it's just a way to sort of walk the  planet a little bit lighter in a way  that's more environmentally conscious  and kind that's a that sort of crafts a  more ethical perspective on on how we  treat our fellow animal friends on the  planet and it also is a very healthy way  to live so for me it's like do the math  like it seemed it's an incredible  lifestyle and if I can go out and and  you know kick ass and Ultraman and do  epic fight I did five Iron Man's on five  wine Islands in under a week you can be  an athlete at the highest level and  still eat this way there's no protein  deficiency issues there's I mean we  could talk for it say you don't get all  the nutrients you need it's it's it's  it's really a fallacy it really is a  fallacy I've never met a doctor who said  I'm treating patients for protein  deficiencies it's just like every food  that you eat has protein in it when you  talk about protein what you're really  talking about is amino acids and  specifically these nine amino acids that  are called the essential amino acids  those are the amino acids that our  bodies cannot synthesize on our own we  have to get them from our food and  people are under the misapprehension  that the only way to get them is through  animal products but the animals get them  through eating plants so I'm just eating  lower on the food chain but doing it 11  years I'm able to I'm 51 years old right  now I go out and I push my body as hard  as anybody out there  I continue to develop as an athlete get  stronger get faster I've never had an  issue building lean muscle mass I hit my  athletic peak at like age 45 so you know  I stand in contradiction to this idea  that you cannot be healthy or thrive or  be at your athletic peak unless you're  eating animal products I think it is a  great fallacy it's a gigantic lie it's a  red herring issue and I invite anybody  who's watching this or listening to try  it for 30 days see how you feel don't  take my word for it do your own  experimentation on yourself  you and your wife Julie are starting a  movement in a way that you're pushing  for the plant-based diet you've  published multiple books and now she's  starting her own line of you can cheese  you can GE cheese products yeah so Julie  came out with a book last year called  this cheese is nuts where she spent two  years perfecting the art of making  non-dairy plant-based cheese's I'm an  artist and a singer and and I just come  into whatever I create in life it  doesn't really matter the medium it's  all the one creative force and really in  cheese because I just went into this  like a child trying things and making  stuff up I knew that I would create some  recipes that were pretty good but I  never imagined that I would create  recipes with this level of taste texture  it's it's shocking that there's no dairy  in these products Julie has really  cracked the code on this like she's come  up with some unbelievable recipes you  try have to say they're like insane yeah  it's like you're like what how is this  how does this not have darian it's  unbelievable so that is a universal  response to everybody who has tasted her  cheese's all the recipes of which you  could find in her book but there's a lot  of people that are just not gonna make  vegan cheese at home so she's like I'm  gonna make a line so she's embarking on  the process of commercializing  some of these cheese products which i  think is really exciting and great and  you know what's interesting about that  is that  you know after our first cookbook came  out the plant power way and we were  thinking what's the next book that we  want to write or that Julie wants to  write there were a lot of people said oh  why don't you do a smoothie book or a  pizza but or something like that she's  like no I want to I want to figure out  how to do unbelievably amazing tasting  cheese because that is the one thing  when you meet people and I meet a lot of  people who I talk to about diet  nutrition they say I'd love to go  plant-based I tried it but like cheese I  just I can't give up cheese cheese is  very difficult to give up it was  difficult for me to give up I understand  that I'm sympathetic to that so we  wanted to basically address that problem  and solve it for them and I think Julie  has done that so it's pretty cool what  would you say is the key element a  successful relationship like that that  you enjoy they have well there's a lot  but I think one of the one of the things  that has been very helpful to us is to  understand that we're each sovereign  individuals I'm not here to complete her  she's not here to complete me I'm not  trying to change her and she's not  trying to change me we have our own  independent lives that and we respect  that and each other and then we can kind  of come together and like I said do this  dance but it's the opposite of when you  see Jerry Maguire and he's like you  complete me like you know what I mean  you your job is to complete yourself mmm  you need to invest in that inside job  that interior journey for yourself so  that you can be the best version of  yourself for your partner what's on the  horizon for ritual I know there's a new  book coming up yes so finding ultra came  out in 2012 but I just spent this past  year completely rewriting the book and  the new second edition revised edition  of that comes out in mid-march March  13th it hits bookstores it's a hundred  pages longer a new forward a brand-new  sixty page chapter that's very  prescriptive I kind of walk people  through all the tools that I relied on  to transform my life in the hopes that  you know that  benefit to somebody who's struggling  there's a recipe section I put in this  cleanse that I did there was a lot of  people who were interested in that and I  just rewrote a lot of the pros that I  wasn't happy with so I'm really excited  about that being out and then Julianne I  have a brand new cookbook called the  plant power way italia that comes out  April 24th and that is inspired by these  retreats that Julie and I do in the  Tuscan countryside where we take groups  of people through a week-long seven-day  transformative excursion and adventure  that has a lot to do with plant-based  nutrition cuisine but also meditation  mindfulness athleticism you know  spiritual development relationships and  the like and the experience of taking  these groups through these experiences  in the Italian countryside has kind of  inspired Julie's acumen in the kitchen  she's developed 125 new plant-based  recipes that are Italian which is pretty  exciting so we're excited about that  yeah so finally what's a message you'd  like to leave the viewers with the first  question that you asked me I would put  that on the audience like who are you  you know who are you like I want people  to really think about that and wrestle  with that and not just say oh I'm a I'm  an entrepreneur I'm an athlete I'm a I'm  a youtube or whatever it is  no that's not the answer who are you who  are what makes you tick what do you care  about what is your unique blueprint what  is your what is it that that that you  can express and manifest in the world  that is uniquely you you know and I  think if you a tune your focus to that  that will unlock doors that will provide  the path forward for what you should do  with your life  do you think it comes with experience  and time or do you think anyone can do  it at any certain point of their life I  think it's helpful if you're in pain  because pain is a great motivator so if  you're suffering and you're unhappy I  would say rather than try to make  yourself feel better  leverage that pain point to do that work  and self improve but you don't have to  be in pain anybody can do make a choice  make a decision it's harder to make  those decisions when things are going  well or you've kind of set up your life  with a lot of distractions but I think  if you can develop the wherewithal the  spine the devotion the dedication the  intention to do that kind of work that  is the path of the master rich you're  officially a legend but now a legendary  legend legendary so thanks my pleasure  thank you so much talk to you appreciate  it thank you so much  [Music]  you
 

